{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.StandardModel.WritheLevelParity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.StandardModel.WritheLevelParity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Int.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Even","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":28,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.leptonNumber_collapses_at_minimal_level","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.leptonNumber_collapses_at_minimal_level","kind":"theorem","line":348,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L348-L379","doc":"**`L = sign(writhe)` does not survive the minimal-level ruling either.**\n\n`ChiralFermionBraidWords.lean` rescued `L` from\n`LeptonNumberCarrierChirality.lean`'s amphichirality obstruction by taking the\ncarrier to be the *braid word* rather than the knot type: `νₑ = σ₁` and\n`ν̄ₑ = σ₁⁻¹` are distinct words, so the mirror acts freely on them even though\nboth close to the amphichiral unknot.\n\nThe owner ruling in `WritheMarkovInvariance.lean` — states are Gröbner–Shirshov\nnormal forms, and the observable is evaluated at the **lowest level of the\ntower** — closes that escape.  The corpus assigns `νₑ = K_{0₁} = T(2,1)`, the\nunknot, whose braid index is `1`; its minimal-level normal form is the *empty*\nword, and so is its mirror's.  At the level where the framework says observables\nlive, the neutrino and the antineutrino are the **same state**, and no function\nof the state can give them `+1` and `−1`.\n\nThe two hypotheses are exactly the two braid-index facts, carried explicitly.\nThe result is that `L` must come from something other than the writhe — the\nMarkov-II failure (`leptonNumber_not_markov_invariant`) and this one leave no\nreading in which `sign(w)` is lepton number.\n\n**Scope-(a)**: `L = sign(writhe)` is refuted under both readings of the gauge\nfreedom.  Lepton number itself, and the `Y = B − L` route, are untouched. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.statistics_reading_fails_on_gluon","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.statistics_reading_fails_on_gluon","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L328-L344","doc":"**Component parity is not fermion/boson statistics.**\n\nThe tempting physical reading — odd component count ↔ fermion — matches the\nleptons (`σ₁`, `σ₁³`: one component, odd) and the Borromean baryon (three\ncomponents, odd), all fermions.  It fails on the gluon unit: `σ₁σ₂⁻¹ ∈ B₃` has\nunderlying permutation a 3-cycle, so its closure is a **single** component,\nhence odd, hence \"fermion\" — but the gluon is a boson.\n\nSo `carrier_component_parity` is a theorem about permutation cycles with no\nframework content, and the statistics reading of it is refuted.  **Scope-(a)**:\nthis refutes *this* reading of component parity, not the possibility that some\nother level-independent datum tracks statistics. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.carrier_component_parity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.carrier_component_parity","kind":"theorem","line":311,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L311-L324","doc":"**The identity `c ≡ w − n (mod 2)` on every carrier the corpus names.**\n\n`νₑ = σ₁`, the Hopf link `σ₁²`, `e⁻ = σ₁³`, the higher generations `σ₁⁵`,\n`σ₁⁷`, the gluon unit and the Borromean baryon.  Both sides are computed; the\nwrithes come from `writhe`, the component counts from `components`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.carrier_components","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.carrier_components","kind":"theorem","line":297,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L297-L309","doc":"**The component counts, computed.**\n\nEach row is evaluated by the kernel from the braid word alone.  The Borromean\ncount `3` is a genuine check: the Borromean rings have three components. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.borromean","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.borromean","kind":"def","line":294,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L294-L295","doc":"The Borromean baryon `𝓑₃ = (σ₁σ₂⁻¹)³ ∈ B₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.gluonUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.gluonUnit","kind":"def","line":291,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L291-L292","doc":"The gluon unit `σ₁σ₂⁻¹ ∈ B₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.sigma1Pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.sigma1Pow","kind":"def","line":288,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L288-L289","doc":"`σ₁ⁿ ∈ B₂`, whose closure is the torus knot/link `T(2, n)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.components","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.components","kind":"def","line":285,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L285-L286","doc":"Components of the closure of `β ∈ B_n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.numCycles","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.numCycles","kind":"def","line":280,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L280-L283","doc":"The number of cycles of `p`, counted by their minima — that is, the number\nof components of the braid closure. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.orbitReachesBelow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.orbitReachesBelow","kind":"def","line":271,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L271-L278","doc":"Does the orbit of `j` under `p` reach an index below `j`?  Iterating `n`\ntimes suffices for a permutation of `n` letters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.permOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.permOf","kind":"def","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L265-L269","doc":"The underlying permutation of a braid word on `n` strands, in one-line\nnotation: generator `σᵢ^{±1}` acts as the transposition of strands `i` and\n`i+1`, independently of the sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.swapAt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.swapAt","kind":"def","line":259,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L259-L263","doc":"Swap the entries at positions `i` and `i+1` of a one-line permutation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.component_parity_of_permSign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.component_parity_of_permSign","kind":"theorem","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L238-L255","doc":"**`(w − n) mod 2` is the component parity of the closure.**\n\nConditional on the classical cycle-sign identity, carried as `hcycle`: the\nunderlying permutation of `β ∈ B_n` is a product of `length β` transpositions\n(one per letter), and a permutation of `n` letters with `c` cycles has sign\n`(−1)^{n−c}`; equating the two exponents gives `length β ≡ n − c (mod 2)`.\nCombined with `writhe_sub_length_even` this yields `c ≡ w − n (mod 2)`.\n\nThe hypothesis is the *only* unproved input, and it is textbook.\n-- Ref: [kassel2008] https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-68548-9 (§1.3, braid\n-- group to symmetric group; §1.1, parity of permutations) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_add_level_not_pair_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_add_level_not_pair_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":229,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L229-L234","doc":"`w + n` is not invariant either. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_sub_level_not_pair_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_sub_level_not_pair_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":221,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L221-L227","doc":"`w − n` — invariant under *positive* stabilisation — is destroyed by the\nnegative one, which shifts it by `−2`.  Only its parity survives. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_not_pair_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_not_pair_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L214-L219","doc":"The writhe itself is not invariant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.markovPair_of_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.markovPair_of_even","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L203-L210","doc":"**Completeness.**  `(w − n) mod 2` is *the* invariant of the level pair:\ntwo pairs are Markov-equivalent exactly when their parities agree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.markovPair_of_even_of_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.markovPair_of_even_of_le","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L186-L201","doc":"**Completeness, upward case.**  If `n ≤ n'` and the two pairs have the same\n`(w − n)` parity, they are Markov-equivalent: climb to level `n'`, then shift\nthe writhe by the residual even amount. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.markovPair_parity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.markovPair_parity","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L128-L142","doc":"**Soundness: `(w − n) mod 2` is Markov-invariant.**\n\nPositive stabilisation leaves `w − n` alone outright; negative stabilisation\nshifts it by `−2`.  Either way the parity is untouched. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.destab","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.destab","kind":"ctor","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L125-L126","doc":"Negative stabilisation `β ↦ βσₙ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.stab","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.stab","kind":"ctor","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L123-L124","doc":"Positive stabilisation `β ↦ βσₙ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.trans","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.trans","kind":"ctor","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L122-L122","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.symm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.symm","kind":"ctor","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L121-L121","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.refl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair.refl","kind":"ctor","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L120-L120","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.MarkovPair","kind":"inductive","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L115-L126","doc":"The equivalence generated by the two Markov-II moves on the level pair\n`(writhe, strand number)`.  Markov I acts trivially on the pair (it changes\nneither coordinate, by `WritheMarkovInvariance.writhe_conj`), so it contributes\nno generator here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_sub_length_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe_sub_length_even","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L86-L111","doc":"**`writhe β ≡ length β (mod 2)`.**\n\nEvery letter of a braid word is a nonzero generator, so contributes `±1` to the\nwrithe and `1` to the length; `±1 − 1 ∈ {0, −2}` is even.  This is the one\nframework-side step of the bridge to component parity, and it is unconditional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.writhe","kind":"def","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L81-L82","doc":"The writhe: the sum of the crossing signs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.BraidWord","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.LevelParity.BraidWord","kind":"def","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/WritheLevelParity.lean#L77-L79","doc":"A braid word: entry `±i` denotes `σᵢ^{±1}`.  Same convention as\n`ChiralFermionBraidWords.lean` and `WritheMarkovInvariance.lean`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}